Window policy of the host with a 16:9 camera, see test_2819 and #2819

A sphere is rendered through a 16:9 camera (36x20.25 mm aperture) in a 160x120 render buffer, so the camera is wider
than the display window. The render pass window policy is the camera's (fit), then crop, match vertically, match
horizontally and don't conform, then fit with an overscan framing and crop with a film translate framing. Each image
must match CameraUtilFraming::ApplyToProjectionMatrix with that window policy, what Storm draws. A render settings
resolution ignores the host framing and its window policy, the camera matching its horizontal aperture as before (husk
relies on it), and clearing it brings the window policy back.
The scenario only runs with the HdArnoldRendererPlugin render delegate driven like a Hydra host, see
testsuite/common/interactive_usd_test.h. Each render is checked in test.cpp, and the crop render (step 1) is compared
against the reference image.

author: cyril.pichard

PARAMS: {'program_env': 'interactive_usd'}
